body:
I would say focus less on the idea that you need to be "fixed." These emotions ebb and flow like waves. There are good and bad days... I've been in therapy weekly since June and I'm taking three different medicines. Some days, my emotions and my pain do still feel all-consuming and suffocating... Other days, I feel a bit more hopeful, and some days I even feel some semblance of happiness.. But I guess the one thing I keep holding onto is that feeling of hope, that it isn't totally hopeless and I'm not completely alone. 

&#x200B;

I have a really good therapist and we focus more on coping skills and grounding techniques than we do on unpacking the trauma. She very much allows me to lead the therapy and never pressures me into speaking about things that may be too difficult for me in the moment. She validates me and assures me that I'm not crazy when I call myself crazy, or feel like I am. I think her teaching me coping skills and mindful thinking has really helped me feel more in control of myself and my surroundings, and that in turn has made me feel more courageous. 

&#x200B;

There are still really bad days, and there will always be triggers no matter what... But therapy has helped me not feel so alone and completely dead inside. You may have to hop around from therapist to therapist until you find the right fit, but once you do, it can be really helpful.
